    "turn_text": "at where we are today. And we have a a government which continues to fall short in meeting the healthcare aspirations of the people. And there's so many people who cannot access healthcare. The people are afraid of going to a hospital because they know they can't pay the bills. We're told that any visit to treat coronavirus could lead the bills as high as $30,000. People are worried about where their next meal is coming from. They're worried about if they're going to have a roof over their head. They're worried about Are they going to have a job again with their wages because, I mean, all these things come into play and then health care, which in every other industrialized democracy is a given is not a given. It's a taken here. It's and what's taken away from people is access is affordability and is the hope of longevity because there's a system is a very cruel system. which keeps people from being able to cover their basic needs with respect to health. But again, what I want to stress here, Ron, is that each of us has a certain responsibility as well. Now, some people are born with conditions that, through no fault of their own, lead them to a life of dependence on health care and of acute dependence on others. I'm not talking about them. Whether it's a moral obligation, we have to care for those who can't care for themselves. We're talking about all of us who make choices every day about what we eat, about what we drink, about the kind of food that we eat that does impact on our health. And this is an appreciation, it's a fact. So, you know, health is a cumulative effect. And a healthy nation needs to emphasize nutrition and diet and exercise and things that can contribute to people's long-term health. Everyone makes different choices. The beauty of our country is we get a chance to do that. But there are consequences, and some people pay dearly for the choices they make. And even for them, we need to",
