  {
    "episode_id": 91237,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91237.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 62,
    "turn_text": "Well, listen, I don't have any problem with millionaires or billionaires or any, you know, my problem with the system. is that the mechanisms do not exist for people to be able to accumulate their own wealth, that the mechanisms in our society accelerate wealth to the top. That does create billionaires, absolutely true. And I recognize that. But at the same time, We've got to be very careful in creating dichotomies like us versus the billionaires that lead us into a philosophical cul-de-sac because the truth is we have to have a culture which teaches people how to achieve wealth. And here, wealth has been achieved through financialization. Wealth is not necessarily achieved through education. although education isn't of itself an important form of wealth. So I want to be, you know, I'm very careful about attacking individuals based on what status they find themselves in and life. But I have no problem at all in saying let's break up the monopolies, which have caused or enabled people to cascade their wealth upwards. Let's have some fair estate taxes. which take the possibility of people passing down generation to generation enormous amounts of wealth in a feudal type system. Let's have a taxation system that's there and causes people to make more of a contribution if they're making more. These are all things that we can handle, but I feel it's risky to just go after people because of their wealth And really, we need to look at the broader questions in our system, which stop people from being able to make a living, which have to do with education, job opportunities, health, a myriad of",
