    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/9/1/91237.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 70,
    "turn_text": "And so we're starting to learn more about what's essential in life. And I'll tell you, I'm not interested in knocking any individuals, because I think that what's called for today is compassion. The decisions that people have to make, they're doing the best they can, and sometimes they're not the right decisions. We all have our own decisions we have to make for ourselves. And so I do. And again, there's a meaning about being as presidents of our own lives, as governors of our own lives. And if our decisions align with people who are in office, fine. But if they don't, that's our freedom to choose. But there are certain realities that we're faced with there about how a disease gets transmitted. about the socialization that we desire, the risk that come with it during a pandemic. And at the same time, I will tell you that my Elizabeth and I feel very blessed. We have more time together than we've ever had. For those who are partnered, it can be challenging the more time you spend with someone. But Elizabeth and I have found that we're in a state of real joy, having a chance to spend even more time with each other. And I wish that everyone listening would have the same experience that we're having, because it's really a deepening of our love. And you find out, when all of a sudden done, we all have opinions about this or that. We all see the world in one way or another. But if you don't have love, it's not worth the heck of a lot.",
